\pard\sa180\f0\fs22\cf1
\pard\sa200\sb100\f0\fs40\b\cf2 CyberFaCES SmartSearch -- Experiment Runbook\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Step-by-step commands to run each of the nine retrieval experiments as isolated Kubernetes Jobs in the cyberfaces-dev namespace on Purdue's Anvil cluster. Every command is copy-pasteable and assumes you are in the repository root.\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Companion to README.md, section "Running the experiments". The setup steps and seven of the nine experiments were executed from this document on 2026-09-16 and produced the expected numbers; see "Verification record" at the end for exactly which, and what is still outstanding.\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 Jobs never run inside the live cyberfaces-rag Deployment. Its 4Gi memory limit OOM-kills (exit 137) when all three reranker models load at once. Nothing in this runbook can affect the production service.\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 Anvil's GPUs are shared with other tenants. Expect to run these ONE AT A TIME, and expect occasional failures that are nobody's fault -- see Troubleshooting, which covers every failure mode encountered so far and what to do about each.\par
\pard\sa140\sb340\f0\fs30\b\cf2 0. One-time setup\b0\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 1 -- confirm cluster access and secrets\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil get ns cyberfaces-dev\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil get secret -n cyberfaces-dev | grep -E 'rag-secrets|hf-token'\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Expect the namespace to be Active, plus two secrets: cyberfaces-rag-secrets (provides ANVILGPT_API) and cyberfaces-hf-token (provides token, used by the gte Jobs). If the HuggingFace one is missing, create it from a free read token at huggingface.co/settings/tokens:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 printf '%s' "hf_xxx..." > /tmp/.hf_token   # never pass a token as a CLI arg\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil create secret generic cyberfaces-hf-token \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   -n cyberfaces-dev --from-file=token=/tmp/.hf_token\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 shred -u /tmp/.hf_token\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Why the file round-trip: --from-literal would leave the token in your shell history and, briefly, in the process list.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 2 -- confirm the course content cloned correctly\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 All three corpora the experiments read are TRACKED in the repository, so a fresh clone needs no extra setup. Just confirm the counts:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 ls courses_md/*.md       | wc -l   # 346  -- Experiment 2\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 ls summaries             | wc -l   # 346  -- Experiment 3\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 ls curated_summaries_35q | wc -l   # 239  -- Experiments 4, 5, 6\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Two things are deliberately NOT tracked, and neither is needed to run anything: courses/ (2.1 GB of raw source attachments, from which courses_md/ is generated by extract_courses_full.py) and courses_md/.cache/ (regenerable OCR and network cache). To rebuild the corpora from scratch, that is what extract_courses_full.py, make_module_summaries.py and generate_descriptions.py are for.\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 summaries/ and curated_summaries_35q/ are DIFFERENT corpora with different naming schemes (unit_N__slug.md vs NNN-slug.md) and zero overlapping files. Mixing them up silently breaks Experiments 4-6, and has done.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 3 -- define the wait helper\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 This is a shell function, not a command. Paste it once per terminal session, or add it to ~/.zshrc. Every experiment below uses it.\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 wait_job () \{\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   until kubectl --context=anvil get job "$1" -n cyberfaces-dev \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3         -o jsonpath='\{.status.conditions[*].type\}' 2>/dev/null \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3         | grep -qE 'Complete|Failed'; do sleep 20; done\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   kubectl --context=anvil get job "$1" -n cyberfaces-dev\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 \}\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 It polls every 20 seconds and returns when the Job reports either Complete or Failed, then prints the final status line (1/1 = success, 0/1 = failure). Matching BOTH outcomes is deliberate: waiting only for Complete hangs forever on a crash.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 4 -- create today's log directory\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 mkdir -p evaluate/k8s/run_logs_$(date +%F)\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 CRITICAL: capture each Job's logs before the pod is garbage-collected. kubectl logs works on a finished pod; kubectl cp and kubectl exec do NOT. ttlSecondsAfterFinished is 86400 (24h), so results not saved within a day are gone for good. Two early runs were lost exactly this way.\par
\pard\sa140\sb340\f0\fs30\b\cf2 1. Shared ConfigMaps\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Jobs mount their scripts and data from ConfigMaps that live on the cluster with NO TTL, so they can be months older than your working copy. Two of the five known real problems have come from stale copies. Regenerate them at the start of a run.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 1 -- see what is currently there\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil get configmap -n cyberfaces-dev\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 2 -- regenerate cyberfaces-hn-code (Experiments 1, 3, 7, 8, 9)\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil create configmap cyberfaces-hn-code \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=app_main.py=./app/main.py \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=compare_retrievers.py=./evaluate/compare_retrievers.py \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=calculate_ndcg.py=./evaluate/calculate_ndcg.py \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=compare_bm25_expansion.py=./evaluate/compare_bm25_expansion.py \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=expand_ground_truth_pool.py=./evaluate/expand_ground_truth_pool.py \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=compare_hybrid_rerank.py=./evaluate/compare_hybrid_rerank.py \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   -n cyberfaces-dev --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl --context=anvil apply -f -\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 The --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl apply -f - idiom makes this idempotent. Plain kubectl create fails with AlreadyExists on a second run. Note app_main.py=./app/main.py: ConfigMap keys cannot contain a slash, so app/main.py is flattened to app_main.py and the Job puts it back at /work/app/main.py using subPath.\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 apply REPLACES the whole data map -- keys you omit are deleted. Always pass all six --from-file flags.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 3 -- regenerate cyberfaces-hn-data (Experiments 1, 7, 8, 9)\b0\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 This one uses `replace`, not `apply`. Its payload exceeds apply's 256KiB last-applied-configuration annotation cap, and apply will fail outright.\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 gzip -9 -c ./final_evaluation_dataset_1035q.jsonl > /tmp/gt1035.jsonl.gz\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil create configmap cyberfaces-hn-data \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=data.jsonl=./data.jsonl \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=course_unit_map.jsonl=./course_unit_map.jsonl \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=final_evaluation_dataset.jsonl.gz=/tmp/gt1035.jsonl.gz \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   -n cyberfaces-dev --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl --context=anvil replace -f -\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 4 -- verify the right answer key was loaded\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 There are two graded answer keys and they are easy to confuse. Getting this wrong produces no error -- every metric just silently shifts. Always check:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil get configmap cyberfaces-hn-data -n cyberfaces-dev -o json \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   | python3 -c "import json,sys,base64,gzip; \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 raw=gzip.decompress(base64.b64decode(json.load(sys.stdin)['binaryData']['final_evaluation_dataset.jsonl.gz'])); \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 print('rows:', raw.count(b'\\n'))"\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 MUST print 10428 (the 1,035-query set). If it prints 10024, the 996-query set was loaded by mistake and Experiments 1, 6, 7, 8, 9 will all be wrong. This has happened, by following a regeneration comment that named the wrong file.\par
\pard\sa140\sb340\f0\fs30\b\cf2 Experiment 1 -- BM25 vs. vector vs. vector+rerank\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Answer key: 1,035-query. Runtime: about 90 seconds. Needs: cyberfaces-hn-code and cyberfaces-hn-data from section 1.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 1 -- submit\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil delete job cyberfaces-exp1-full -n cyberfaces-dev --ignore-not-found\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil apply -f evaluate/k8s/exp1-job.yaml\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 The delete is not optional if the Job ran within the last 24 hours -- see Troubleshooting, "apply says unchanged".\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 2 -- wait, then capture the log\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 wait_job cyberfaces-exp1-full\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil logs -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=cyberfaces-exp1-full --tail=-1 \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   > evaluate/k8s/run_logs_$(date +%F)/exp1_full_1035q.log\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 tail -7 evaluate/k8s/run_logs_$(date +%F)/exp1_full_1035q.log\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 --tail=-1 is required. With a -l selector, kubectl logs defaults to the last 10 lines only, so without it you silently capture a truncated log.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Expected result\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 BM25 (keyword)       0.4159/0.4212   0.6942   0.2858   0.3645\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 Vector (MPNet)       0.5244/0.5597   0.8265   0.3786   0.4816\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 Vector+Jina rerank   0.4916/0.5246   0.7987   0.3189   0.4086\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 The BM25 row must match to four decimals -- it is fully deterministic. Vector and rerank vary by about 0.001 between runs because the Chroma index is rebuilt and is approximate. Despite the printed label, "Vector (MPNet)" is actually gte-large: app/main.py builds the store with Alibaba-NLP/gte-large-en-v1.5. The label is stale.\par
\pard\sa140\sb340\f0\fs30\b\cf2 Experiment 2 -- full document content, gte-large + jina rerank\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Answer key: 996-query. TWO Jobs -- the semantic baseline and the reranked arm. Needs courses_md/ from the repository, plus the HuggingFace token secret.\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 Run BOTH arms. Comparing the reranked number against the originally published 0.567 compares two different scoring conventions, and that mismatch is what made reranking look like a win.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 1 -- ConfigMap\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 gzip -9 -c ./evaluate/final_evaluation_dataset.jsonl > /tmp/gt996.jsonl.gz\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil create configmap cyberfaces-gte-jina-code \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=compare_gte_vs_jina.py=./evaluate/compare_gte_vs_jina.py \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=calculate_ndcg.py=./evaluate/calculate_ndcg.py \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=ground_truth.jsonl.gz=/tmp/gt996.jsonl.gz \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   -n cyberfaces-dev --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl --context=anvil apply -f -\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 2 -- build the corpus tarball once (used by both arms)\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 tar czf /tmp/courses_md.tar.gz -C . courses_md\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 courses_md/ is about 3MB gzipped, well over the ~1MiB ConfigMap limit, so it cannot be mounted. It is uploaded into the running pod instead, which is why both arms block mid-run waiting for you.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 3 -- semantic baseline (about 50 seconds once uploaded)\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil delete job cyberfaces-gte-baseline -n cyberfaces-dev --ignore-not-found\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil apply -f evaluate/k8s/gte-baseline-job.yaml\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 \par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 until kubectl --context=anvil logs -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=cyberfaces-gte-baseline \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3       --tail=20 2>/dev/null | grep -q WAITING_FOR_UPLOAD; do sleep 5; done\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 \par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 POD=$(kubectl --context=anvil get pods -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=cyberfaces-gte-baseline \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3       --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp -o jsonpath='\{.items[-1:].metadata.name\}')\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil cp /tmp/courses_md.tar.gz cyberfaces-dev/"$POD":/work/courses_md.tar.gz\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil exec -n cyberfaces-dev "$POD" -- touch /work/READY\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 \par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 wait_job cyberfaces-gte-baseline\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil logs -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=cyberfaces-gte-baseline --tail=-1 \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   > evaluate/k8s/run_logs_$(date +%F)/exp2_gte_baseline.log\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 tail -4 evaluate/k8s/run_logs_$(date +%F)/exp2_gte_baseline.log\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Expected: Semantic (gte-large) 0.6346/0.6972, MRR 0.8087, P@10 0.3307, R@10 0.6220, n=960. The log should also say "kept 297/346 modules" and "960 usable queries".\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 4 -- reranked arm (about 20 minutes once uploaded)\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Identical dance, different Job name:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil delete job cyberfaces-gte-jina -n cyberfaces-dev --ignore-not-found\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil apply -f evaluate/k8s/gte-jina-job.yaml\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 \par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 until kubectl --context=anvil logs -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=cyberfaces-gte-jina \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3       --tail=20 2>/dev/null | grep -q WAITING_FOR_UPLOAD; do sleep 5; done\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 \par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 POD=$(kubectl --context=anvil get pods -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=cyberfaces-gte-jina \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3       --sort-by=.metadata.creationTimestamp -o jsonpath='\{.items[-1:].metadata.name\}')\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil cp /tmp/courses_md.tar.gz cyberfaces-dev/"$POD":/work/courses_md.tar.gz\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil exec -n cyberfaces-dev "$POD" -- touch /work/READY\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 \par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 wait_job cyberfaces-gte-jina\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil logs -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=cyberfaces-gte-jina --tail=-1 \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   > evaluate/k8s/run_logs_$(date +%F)/exp2_gte_jina.log\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 tail -4 evaluate/k8s/run_logs_$(date +%F)/exp2_gte_jina.log\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Expected: Cross-enc (jina-v3) 0.6227/0.6738, MRR 0.8131, P@10 0.2902, R@10 0.5613, n=960. Compared like-for-like against Step 3, reranking LOSES on NDCG@10, precision and recall.\par
\pard\sa140\sb340\f0\fs30\b\cf2 Experiment 3 -- query rewriting\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Answer key: 996-query. Uses summaries/ from the repository. Runtime: historically about 6 hours, but see the note -- the cause has been fixed and it should now be far faster.\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 Experiment 3 CANNOT be moved to the 1,035-query key. query_comparison.xlsx supplies the old->new rewrite mapping, and it covers 996/996 queries on the 996-query key but only 17/1,035 on the other. The two keys share just 17 query strings and are not nested.\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 The 6-hour figure was a device bug, not real work: compare_rewrites_summaries.py selected "mps" or "cpu" and never checked CUDA, so gte-large embedded on two CPU cores of an H100 node. That line has since been fixed to prefer CUDA, matching compare_gte_vs_jina.py. The runtime should drop to minutes, but this has NOT been confirmed by a completed run -- every attempt so far has been blocked by GPU contention. Treat the runtime as unknown-but-much-lower, and the expected numbers below as coming from the original CPU run.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 1 -- code ConfigMap\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil create configmap cyberfaces-exp3-code \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=compare_query_rewrites.py=./evaluate/compare_query_rewrites.py \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=compare_rewrites_summaries.py=./evaluate/compare_rewrites_summaries.py \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   -n cyberfaces-dev --dry-run=client -o yaml | kubectl --context=anvil apply -f -\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 2 -- data ConfigMap\b0\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 This one needs delete + plain create. The binary payload is too large for apply's annotation cap, and replace would also carry it.\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 tar czf /tmp/summaries.tar.gz summaries\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 gzip -9 -c ./evaluate/final_evaluation_dataset.jsonl > /tmp/gt996.jsonl.gz\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil delete configmap cyberfaces-exp3-data -n cyberfaces-dev --ignore-not-found\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil create configmap cyberfaces-exp3-data \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=summaries.tar.gz=/tmp/summaries.tar.gz \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=query_comparison.xlsx=./query_comparison.xlsx \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --from-file=gt996.jsonl.gz=/tmp/gt996.jsonl.gz \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   -n cyberfaces-dev\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 3 -- run\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil delete job cyberfaces-exp3-rewrites -n cyberfaces-dev --ignore-not-found\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil apply -f evaluate/k8s/exp3-job.yaml\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 wait_job cyberfaces-exp3-rewrites\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil logs -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=cyberfaces-exp3-rewrites --tail=-1 \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   > evaluate/k8s/run_logs_$(date +%F)/exp3_rewrites.log\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Expected result\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Plain corpus (n=996): bm25 old 0.6799 / new 0.4441; vector old 0.8344 / new 0.5079. Summaries corpus (n=979): gte old 0.6250 / new 0.5622. Rewriting loses in every case -- the most robust conclusion in the whole study, holding under both scoring conventions, both embedding models and both corpora.\par
\pard\sa140\sb340\f0\fs30\b\cf2 Experiments 4 and 5 -- text formatting and curated summaries\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Answer key: 35-query hand-labeled. One Job produces both experiments. Runtime about 4 minutes. This is the lightest experiment -- a good first one to try if the cluster is busy.\par
