\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 original   0.4159/0.4212   0.6942   0.2858   0.3645\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 expanded   0.4469/0.4570   0.7168   0.3112   0.3978\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Expansion should win on all four metrics. The "original" row must equal Experiment 9's BM25-only row and Experiment 1's BM25 row exactly -- that three-way agreement is the main cross-check that nothing has drifted.\par
\pard\sa140\sb340\f0\fs30\b\cf2 Experiment 8 -- de-biasing the answer key\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Answer key: 1,035-query, pooled up to 22,124 rows during the run. Runtime about 38 minutes. Needs cyberfaces-hn-code and cyberfaces-hn-data from section 1.\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 Do NOT split the pooling and comparison into separate Jobs. The pooled answer key exists only on the pod's filesystem and dies with it; the manifest chains both steps into one command deliberately. Splitting them lost the results twice.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 1 -- run\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil delete job cyberfaces-hn-pool -n cyberfaces-dev --ignore-not-found\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil apply -f evaluate/k8s/hn-pool-job.yaml\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 wait_job cyberfaces-hn-pool\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil logs -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=cyberfaces-hn-pool --tail=-1 \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   > evaluate/k8s/run_logs_$(date +%F)/exp8_hn_pool.log\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 tail -10 evaluate/k8s/run_logs_$(date +%F)/exp8_hn_pool.log\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Expected result -- read this before comparing numbers\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Experiment 8 is the ONE experiment whose absolute numbers should not be expected to match, because it rebuilds its own answer key on every run. Confirm the inputs first:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 1035 queries, ground truth has 10428 existing judged rows\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 That line must appear -- it is the check that the correct 1,035-query key was loaded. Then check how many judge calls failed:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 grep -c 'judge FAILED' evaluate/k8s/run_logs_$(date +%F)/exp8_hn_pool.log\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 The judge (gemma4:26b-a4b) runs at temperature 0 and the candidate pool is deterministic, so a clean run grades about 11,900 net-new rows. Every failed judge call silently DROPS a row, shrinking the pooled key and shifting every downstream score. Two observed runs:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 date     judge FAILED   new rows   pooled key   Vector    Vector+rerank\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 ------------------------------------------------------------------------\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 09-09         200         11696       22124      0.5665       0.5185\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 09-16           0         11900       22328      0.5968       0.5489\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Prefer a run with 0 judge failures -- it grades the complete pool and is the more trustworthy of the two. Note that the originally published figures (0.5665 / 0.5185) come from the run that lost 200 rows.\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 WHAT MUST HOLD, regardless of the row count: vector-only beats vector+rerank on ALL FOUR metrics. That is the finding, and it reproduced identically in both runs -- the point of the experiment is that de-biasing the answer key does not change who wins.\par
\pard\sa140\sb340\f0\fs30\b\cf2 Experiment 9 -- production's real candidate pool\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Answer key: 1,035-query. Runtime about 24 minutes. Needs cyberfaces-hn-code and cyberfaces-hn-data from section 1.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Step 1 -- run\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil delete job cyberfaces-hybrid-rerank -n cyberfaces-dev --ignore-not-found\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil apply -f evaluate/k8s/hybrid-rerank-job.yaml\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 wait_job cyberfaces-hybrid-rerank\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil logs -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=cyberfaces-hybrid-rerank --tail=-1 \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   > evaluate/k8s/run_logs_$(date +%F)/exp9_hybrid_rerank.log\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 tail -10 evaluate/k8s/run_logs_$(date +%F)/exp9_hybrid_rerank.log\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Expected result\b0\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 BM25 only                    0.4159/0.4212   0.6942   0.2858   0.3645\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 Vector only                  0.5241/0.5594   0.8265   0.3785   0.4813\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 BM25+Vector -> Jina rerank   0.5065/0.5436   0.7972   0.3369   0.4327\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\sb340\f0\fs30\b\cf2 Troubleshooting\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Every failure mode encountered while running these experiments, and what to do about each. The first three are by far the most common.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 "job.batch/NAME unchanged" after apply\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 The Job already exists and matches the manifest, so apply did nothing -- it did NOT start a new run. Job specs are largely immutable and the Job lingers for 24 hours after finishing (ttlSecondsAfterFinished), so this is exactly the window where you hit it. Delete first:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil delete job NAME -n cyberfaces-dev\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil apply -f evaluate/k8s/THE-JOB.yaml\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Every experiment above already includes the delete for this reason.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Pod stuck in ContainerCreating for several minutes\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Almost always the image pull. The image is several GB of GPU/ML dependencies, and a node that has not run these Jobs before takes roughly 6-7 minutes to fetch it. Check:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 POD=$(kubectl --context=anvil get pods -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=THE-LABEL \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3       -o jsonpath='\{.items[-1:].metadata.name\}')\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil describe pod -n cyberfaces-dev "$POD" | sed -n '/^Events:/,$p'\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 A "Pulling image" event with no "Pulled" yet means wait. imagePullPolicy is IfNotPresent, so the next Job landing on that node starts in seconds.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 No progress lines for 20+ minutes after the pod starts Running\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Usually the model download, not a hang. On a node whose HuggingFace cache is cold, the Jobs fetch about 2.9 GB (MiniLM plus jina-reranker-v3, or gte-large) before the first query runs. Observed 22 minutes for Experiment 8 on 2026-09-16, with the log sitting on a trust_remote_code notice the whole time, and the first checkpoint appearing immediately afterwards at the normal rate.\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Confirm it is downloading rather than stuck by watching the cache grow:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 POD=$(kubectl --context=anvil get pods -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=THE-LABEL -o jsonpath='\{.items[-1:].metadata.name\}')\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil exec -n cyberfaces-dev "$POD" -- du -sh /root/.cache/huggingface\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Growing = downloading, wait. Stable at ~2.9 GB = finished, and the first checkpoint is imminent. Do not inspect /proc/1 to judge this: PID 1 is the shell wrapper, not the Python process, so its CPU counters stay near zero and look like a hang. The log and the cache size are the reliable signals.\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 Only gte-jina-job.yaml, gte-baseline-job.yaml and exp3-job.yaml wire in the cyberfaces-hf-token secret. The other Jobs download anonymously and are therefore exposed to HuggingFace throttling, which is what makes a cold-cache start slow. Adding the same two env vars (HF_TOKEN and HUGGING_FACE_HUB_TOKEN, from cyberfaces-hf-token) to the remaining manifests would remove that exposure. Not done yet -- the runs verified here used the manifests as committed.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Pod Pending, "Insufficient nvidia.com/gpu"\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Every GPU-capable node is busy. There are 9 schedulable GPU nodes and they are shared with other tenants; during one full run all 9 were occupied the entire time.\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf5 GOOD: \b0 This self-heals. The Job waits in the scheduler queue indefinitely -- no activeDeadlineSeconds is set -- and starts automatically the moment a slice frees. Leave it alone; do not delete and resubmit.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 torch.OutOfMemoryError: CUDA out of memory\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Different from the above, and it does NOT self-heal. The scheduler DID give you a GPU, but another tenant is occupying that physical device's memory. Read the numbers in the error before assuming your Job is at fault:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 GPU 0 has a total capacity of 79.18 GiB of which 262.94 MiB is free.\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 Including non-PyTorch memory, this process has 4.24 GiB memory in use.\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 79.18 GiB total, 0.26 GiB free, your process holding only 4.24 GiB -- so roughly 74.7 GiB belongs to somebody else. Nothing in your configuration causes or prevents this.\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 It can strike at either of two points, and the traceback tells you which: during corpus embedding (the frame names Alibaba-NLP/new-impl/modeling.py, i.e. gte-large) or later when the jina reranker runs (the frame names transformers/models/qwen3/modeling_qwen3.py, jina-reranker-v3's Qwen3 backbone). Both were seen on 2026-09-16 within an hour of each other. The cause and the fix are identical either way.\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 backoffLimit is 0, so the Job will NOT retry. It ends as 0/1 Error and stays that way until the 24h TTL removes it. Nothing is queued and nothing is waiting.\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Retry by hand. Failure is cheap: the OOM hits during corpus embedding, about 60-90 seconds in, and by then the image is cached.\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil delete job NAME -n cyberfaces-dev\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil apply -f evaluate/k8s/THE-JOB.yaml\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 wait_job NAME\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 To confirm it cleared the hazard, watch for the log to get PAST the embedding step -- "Loading BM25 index..." appearing after "Loading vector DB + documents..." means you are through:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil logs -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=THE-LABEL -f --tail=20\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 If you would rather Kubernetes retry for you, change backoffLimit: 0 to backoffLimit: 6 in the manifest. It then recreates the pod on failure with exponential backoff (10s, 20s, 40s, 80s, 160s, 320s), roughly ten minutes of automatic retrying. These experiments are deterministic and write fresh output each run, so a retry is safe; Experiments 7 and 8 additionally checkpoint and will resume rather than restart.\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 PYTORCH_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True, which the error message suggests, does NOT help here. It mitigates fragmentation within your own allocation, but you are not fragmented -- you are starved. No allocator tuning closes a 263 MiB / 494 MiB gap.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Job reports 1/1 Complete but a method's NDCG is nan\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 The most dangerous failure mode, because the Job looks successful. ndcg_eval.py isolates errors per query -- added so one transient AnvilGPT hiccup cannot abort a 23-minute run. Under GPU pressure that same isolation swallows CUDA OOMs: every CrossEncoder_advance query fails, the run continues to the end, and the Job exits 1/1 with a meaningless advance row.\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 The tell is in the progress lines. Healthy:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   [20/1035] elapsed=25s rrf_mean=0.437 adv_mean=0.436 rrf_errors=0 adv_errors=0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Broken -- observed on 2026-09-16:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   [140/1035] elapsed=140s rrf_mean=0.389 adv_mean=nan rrf_errors=0 adv_errors=140\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 adv_errors climbing one-for-one with the query counter, adv_mean=nan, rrf_errors staying 0. RRF is unaffected because it never loads jina-reranker-v3; only the advance path makes the large per-query allocations (600-1000 MiB) that fail when another tenant holds the GPU.\par
\pard\sa140\li240\f0\fs21\b\cf4 NOTE: \b0 ALWAYS read the n_errors column in the final table -- the Job's exit status does not reflect it. But read it with the right threshold, below: a handful of errors is normal, wholesale errors mean the run is worthless.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 How many n_errors is too many?\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Distinguish two very different situations:\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 WHOLESALE FAILURE -- n_errors tracks the query counter one-for-one and the mean is nan. The method never worked. Discard and resubmit. This is the case described above.\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 ISOLATED TRANSIENT ERRORS -- a handful out of 1,035, mean is a normal number. These are expected: the scripts deliberately isolate per-query failures so one transient AnvilGPT or CUDA hiccup cannot abort a long run. The run is usable. The original published work recorded exactly this -- its Experiment 6 table has a CrossEncoder_advance row reading 995 scored, 39 excluded, 1 error. The 2026-09-16 verification run saw 16 errors out of 1,035 (983 scored, 36 excluded) and still landed on 0.447 mean, matching the documented value.\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 So: note the count, because n_scored and n_excluded shift with it, and prefer a clean 0-error run when you can get one. Do not throw away a run over a few isolated errors.\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Fix is the same as a plain CUDA OOM: delete and resubmit when the GPU has room. Check the FIRST progress line rather than waiting for the end -- if adv_errors is already 20 at query 20, abort immediately instead of burning 23 minutes:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil logs -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=cyberfaces-eval-ndcg \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --tail=-1 | grep -E '^  \\[[0-9]+/'\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Experiment 7 is the lightest GPU consumer -- compare_bm25_expansion.py builds its retrievers with need_reranker=False, so it never loads jina at all. If the cluster is under pressure, it is the one most likely to get through.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 "missing the kubectl.kubernetes.io/last-applied-configuration annotation"\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 A warning, not an error, and it is safe to ignore. It appears the first time you apply over a ConfigMap that was originally made with plain kubectl create. The annotation is patched automatically and later applies are silent. The command still succeeds -- look for the "configmap/NAME configured" line after it.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 "metadata.annotations: Too long: must have at most 262144 bytes"\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 The ConfigMap payload exceeds apply's 256KiB annotation cap. Use replace instead, or delete then plain create. This affects cyberfaces-hn-data (use replace) and cyberfaces-exp3-data (use delete + create). Both are already written that way above.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 "wait_job: command not found"\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 It is a shell function, not a program. Paste the definition from section 0, step 3 into your current shell -- it does not persist across terminal sessions unless you add it to ~/.zshrc.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 The log looks truncated / only ten lines\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 You omitted --tail=-1. With a -l label selector, kubectl logs defaults to the last 10 lines. If the pod still exists, just re-run the capture with --tail=-1.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Numbers do not match the expected values\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 First decide whether the row is deterministic. RRF, BM25, vector and CrossEncoder_base reproduce exactly. Anything using AnvilGPT -- CrossEncoder_advance, Experiment 7's expansion, Experiment 8's grading -- varies between runs; judge those on direction and rough magnitude.\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 If a DETERMINISTIC row has moved, that is a real finding, not something to overwrite. Check in this order: (1) does the answer-key row count still read 10428, per section 1 step 4; (2) do Experiments 1, 7 and 9 still agree on BM25 = 0.4159/0.4212; (3) has data.jsonl changed, which would mean the course catalog itself moved. Report it rather than adjusting the expected values.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Cross-checks worth running deliberately\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Experiments 1, 7 and 9 each compute BM25 on the same answer key and must agree to four decimals on all four metrics. Experiment 1's vector row should match Experiment 9's to within about 0.001 -- small differences come from rebuilding the approximate Chroma index. Experiment 8's pooling must print exactly 10428 existing and 11696 new rows.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 A run is far slower than the stated runtime\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 GPU sharing costs you two different things. Memory starvation makes runs FAIL (the OOM cases above). Compute sharing just makes them SLOW -- the run is correct, it simply takes several times longer. Experiment 6 was observed at 1.3s per query on a quiet GPU and 8s per query on a busy one, turning 23 minutes into over two hours.\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Do not kill a slow run. Confirm it is healthy instead: the query counter should keep advancing and the error counters should stay at 0. Only the error counters indicate a real problem. The same applies to AnvilGPT latency, which varies independently of the GPU and moved Experiment 7 between 25 and 61 minutes.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 A run that STARTED under contention stays slow -- restart it\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 A pod that launches while another tenant holds most of the GPU gets squeezed into a small allocation, repeatedly hits near-OOM, and leaves PyTorch's caching allocator badly fragmented. That fragmentation persists even after the other tenant leaves, because large allocations in a fragmented pool trigger synchronizing cudaFree/cudaMalloc cycles. Measured on 2026-09-16 for Experiment 6:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 started under contention : 14.24 s/query  (GPU-side residual ~10 s)\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 restarted, GPU now free  :  4.15 s/query  (GPU-side residual ~0.5 s)\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 uncontended baseline     :  1.31 s/query\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Counter-intuitively, killing a healthy-but-slow run and resubmitting can FINISH SOONER. In that case 535 remaining queries at 14.24 s was about 2h05, while a fresh run of all 1,035 at 4.15 s was about 1h12. Check the marginal rate before deciding:\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 But a restart only buys back the START rate, because the fragmentation is PROGRESSIVE, not merely inherited. The restarted run above began at 4.15 s/query and had degraded to 8.47 s/query by query 360, on an idle GPU with 52 GiB free and AnvilGPT measured at 2.2 s -- leaving about 6.3 s/query of GPU-side cost with no external cause. The allocator had grown to 29 GiB of cache by then. So restarting repeatedly is not a fix; it just resets the clock.\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 The actual lever for this is PYTORCH_ALLOC_CONF=expandable_segments:True, added to the Job's env. It lets the allocator grow segments in place instead of accumulating unusable holes. Note the asymmetry, which is easy to get backwards: this setting helps the progressive SLOWDOWN, but does nothing for the hard OOM described earlier -- there you are starved of memory by another tenant, and no allocator tuning conjures free space. Same flag, opposite verdicts, depending on which problem you have.\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil logs -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=THE-LABEL --tail=-1 \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   | grep -E 'elapsed=' | tail -3\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Divide the change in elapsed by the change in the query counter. If it is several times the stated baseline and the GPU is now free, restart.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Telling GPU contention apart from AnvilGPT latency\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 The two look identical from the outside -- both just make runs slow -- but they need different responses. Check the GPU from inside the running pod:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 POD=$(kubectl --context=anvil get pods -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=THE-LABEL \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3       -o jsonpath='\{.items[-1:].metadata.name\}')\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil exec -n cyberfaces-dev "$POD" -- nvidia-smi \\\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   --query-gpu=memory.total,memory.used,memory.free,utilization.gpu --format=csv\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 If memory.used is close to memory.total but your own process accounts for only a few GiB, another tenant is present. If memory.used is essentially all yours and there is plenty free, the GPU is not the problem -- time the API instead:\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 kubectl --context=anvil exec -n cyberfaces-dev "$POD" -- python -c "\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 import os,time,requests\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 k=os.environ['ANVILGPT_API']\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 url='https://anvilgpt.rcac.purdue.edu/api/chat/completions'\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 for i in range(3):\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3     t=time.time()\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3     r=requests.post(url,headers=\{'Authorization':f'Bearer \{k\}','Content-Type':'application/json'\},\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3         json=\{'model':'gpt-oss:120b','messages':[\{'role':'user','content':'reply ok'\}]\},timeout=120)\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3     print(f'\{time.time()-t:.2f\}s \{r.status_code\}')"\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Baseline is about 1.0s per call. Observed 3.0-4.8s on 2026-09-16, which alone accounts for roughly 3.6s of every query in Experiments 6, 7 and 8. Nothing can be done about AnvilGPT latency except wait for it to recover -- but knowing that is the cause saves you from pointlessly resubmitting.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Automating retries through a busy period\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 When another tenant is holding the GPU intermittently, this loop submits, checks the first progress line, and backs off if the run is already erroring -- so you are not sitting at the terminal resubmitting by hand. Shown for Experiment 6; adapt the Job name and the progress pattern for others.\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 for attempt in 1 2 3 4 5 6; do\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   echo "=== attempt $attempt at $(date +%H:%M:%S) ==="\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   kubectl --context=anvil delete job cyberfaces-eval-ndcg -n cyberfaces-dev --ignore-not-found\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   kubectl --context=anvil apply -f evaluate/k8s/ndcg-job.yaml\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   until kubectl --context=anvil logs -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=cyberfaces-eval-ndcg\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3         --tail=-1 2>/dev/null | grep -qE '20/1035'; do sleep 15; done\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   LINE=$(kubectl --context=anvil logs -n cyberfaces-dev -l app=cyberfaces-eval-ndcg\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3          --tail=-1 2>/dev/null | grep -E '20/1035' | head -1)\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   echo "  $LINE"\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   if echo "$LINE" | grep -q 'adv_errors=0'; then\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3     echo '  --> clean, letting it run'; break\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   fi\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   kubectl --context=anvil delete job cyberfaces-eval-ndcg -n cyberfaces-dev --ignore-not-found\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3   sleep 300\par
\pard\li360\sa0\f1\fs18\cf3 done\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Each probe costs about a minute, so this is far cheaper than discovering after 23 minutes that every query errored. Note the two kubectl lines inside are shown wrapped for width -- join each back onto one line when pasting.\par
\pard\sa100\sb240\f0\fs24\b\cf1 Suggested order if the cluster is busy\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 Fastest first, so you find problems early and use short GPU windows: Experiment 1 (90s), Experiment 2's baseline arm (50s), Experiments 4 and 5 (4 min), Experiment 2's reranked arm (20 min), Experiment 6 (23 min), Experiment 9 (24 min), Experiment 7 (25 min), Experiment 8 (38 min), Experiment 3 last (historically 6 hours, expected much less now that its CUDA fix is in).\par
\pard\sa140\sb340\f0\fs30\b\cf2 Verification record\b0\par
\pard\sa140\f0\fs22\cf1 What was actually run from this document, on Anvil, on 2026-09-16. The setup steps in sections 0 and 1 were all executed, including every ConfigMap regeneration command and the answer-key row-count check, which printed 10428 as documented.\par
